# Standard randomness surface.
#
# Design (slice 45m): randomness is a TOOL, never a builtin — the
# same decision as std/time.cor and for the same reason: tool
# calls are traced and substituted under replay, so a program that
# drew 0.42 draws 0.42 again on every re-run. The checker rejects
# tool calls inside `@deterministic` bodies, so a "deterministic"
# agent that secretly rolls dice is a compile error, not a
# production incident.
#
# The pure math methods (`abs`, `min`, `pow`, `sqrt`, ...) live on
# the builtin-method table — see docs/book/05-types.md.

effect rand_draw:
    reversible: true

# Uniform draw in [0.0, 1.0). Nondeterministic; replay substitutes
# the recorded value.
public tool random_float() -> Float uses rand_draw

# Uniform integer in [min, max] — INCLUSIVE on both ends (Python's
# randint, the least surprising contract for "give me 1..6").
# min > max is a runtime error.
public tool random_int(min: Int, max: Int) -> Int uses rand_draw
